<main>
<section class="intro"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="eyebrow">Continuous learning · applied knowledge</div>
  <h1>I learn to <em>build, test and understand.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">This is not a collection of certificates for decoration. It is a record of subjects I have deliberately studied across search, analytics, advertising, e-commerce, content and commercial skills, alongside evidence from projects where I applied what I learned.</p>
  <div class="principles">
    <div class="principle"><strong>Curiosity first</strong><span>I tend to learn because I am trying to understand a system, solve a problem or build something new.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><strong>Technical + commercial</strong><span>My learning crosses analytics, search, advertising, content, e-commerce and business rather than staying inside one narrow discipline.</span></div>
    <div class="principle"><strong>Applied, not theoretical</strong><span>The final section includes historical performance evidence from previous Instagram accounts I built and grew.</span></div>
  </div>
</div></section>
<section class="content-section" id="learning"><div class="wrap">
  <header class="section-head"><div><div class="number">00 · THINGS I KEEP LEARNING</div><h2>Subjects I naturally keep coming back to.</h2></div><p>I am most interested in subjects where technology, behaviour, data and strategy overlap. These are interests, not claims that I have mastered every area.</p></header>
  <div class="learning-grid">
    <article class="learning-card"><div class="learning-index">01</div><h3>AI</h3><p>How AI systems change research, discovery, workflows and what people expect from search.</p></article>
    <article class="learning-card"><div class="learning-index">02</div><h3>AEO</h3><p>How answers are structured so information is easier for answer engines to understand and surface.</p></article>
    <article class="learning-card"><div class="learning-index">03</div><h3>GEO</h3><p>How brands, entities and useful source material can become easier for generative systems to understand.</p></article>
    <article class="learning-card"><div class="learning-index">04</div><h3>SEO</h3><p>Search behaviour, technical foundations, content structure, intent and discoverability.</p></article>
    <article class="learning-card"><div class="learning-index">05</div><h3>Reading data</h3><p>Looking for what the numbers are actually saying before deciding what to change.</p></article>
    <article class="learning-card"><div class="learning-index">06</div><h3>Customer behaviour</h3><p>Why people search, click, compare, trust, hesitate and choose.</p></article>
    <article class="learning-card"><div class="learning-index">07</div><h3>Strategy</h3><p>Connecting individual actions to a wider objective instead of optimising isolated tasks.</p></article>
    <article class="learning-card"><div class="learning-index">08</div><h3>Pattern recognition</h3><p>Finding repeated signals, anomalies and relationships across results, behaviour and systems.</p></article>
    <article class="learning-card"><div class="learning-index">09</div><h3>Psychology</h3><p>Attention, trust, motivation, decision-making and how people interpret information.</p></article>
    <article class="learning-card"><div class="learning-index">10</div><h3>Trends</h3><p>Watching how technology, platforms and behaviour shift over time and what those shifts may change.</p></article>
  </div>
</div></section>
<section class="content-section" id="google"><div class="wrap">
  <header class="section-head"><div><div class="number">01 · HIGHEST RELEVANCE</div><h2>Google certificates.</h2></div><p>Search, advertising, analytics and campaign-platform training shown first because these credentials are the most directly relevant to SEO, measurement and digital acquisition work.</p></header>
